            <ArticleTitle>Factors associated with healthcare utilization among children with noncardiac chest pain and innocent heart murmurs.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>817-25</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1093/jpepsy/jss055</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To examine differences in factors related to health care utilization (HCU) among children eventually diagnosed with noncardiac chest pain (NCCP) or an innocent heart murmur (IHM).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">67 pediatric patients with NCCP and 62 with IHM and their parent/guardian completed paper-and-pencil measures of psychological functioning and past HCU during an initial visit to the cardiologist's office.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Children with NCCP utilized significantly more health care services compared to their IHM counterparts in the year prior to their cardiology visit. Children in the NCCP group had higher internalizing and somatic symptoms, and their parents experienced more anxious symptoms, than those in the IHM group. For the NCCP group only, child and parent psychological symptoms and parent HCU were positively related to child HCU.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Results identify possible child and parent psychological factors that may be the focus of interventions to reduce high rates of HCU among children with NCCP.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
